okay let me think out loud for this one. I think I want to implement a search functionality here for the genres. I could add regex based search, but I am adding a simple search because I am learning right now. The plan is to first create an input element to take user input, and then run some blackbox function on it. This blackbox function would take in the user's query in real time, and have a dictionary or something to look things up, and would return the genres to the user based on the toLowerCase-ed query.
So the first step is to set up an HTML input element and design it according to my will. I might want to do this in a separate file, search.svelte so that I have everything separately for tidiness. 
Interesting, I am done with the input element and its styling. I was having an issue where w-full would make it acquire the entire screen's width, so I added an empty div around it with the right margin classes. I think it looks alright now. I have also added a border-emerald-500 on focus, I think it pairs well with the overall white+black+emerald thing I am going on, because I love Pokemon Emerald.
The next step is clearly drilling some bindings, I think that's what I will do later after taking a shower.
I am back. Time to drill some docs now.
I am done. It turns out that binding components might not be the best pattern for this, but it's still idiomatic. I will be making two commits next - one for the callback pattern and one for the two-way data flow binding pattern. I will make the search work with both for understanding.
Hmm for my search I now have a setup where I am console logging each search, so this pattern works. I don't know what to do right now - I can't really search through stuff as I don't have dummy data. I could pass each genre a name for example and try to see if my search really works. I think that's what I will do.
Okay. Now I have a mock genre array in globals.svelte.ts and I have imported that. Now I have also implemented the search functionality. To do this I used a $derived rune. Why did I do that? Because I wanted something to be in sync with the current state, and using an effect to do that is an antipattern. So yes, I have the derived array of genres. I will now do something where I have an {#each} block where I will write the markup for the genres. I will be using the filtered array for this, and to be completely transparent this was pointed out to me by Claude. I was trying to make it so such that I had an "if filtered query is truthy show everything else show filtered" but it turns out that the filtered array at the base case (that is, no user input) still contains the entire genre list itself. This is because '' is truthy and this is how array.filter() works. So yes, now my pattern is simpler - I could just use an each block with the filtered list itself and render the genres. Let me do that real quick.
I am done! The search works now! This is the first time I have implemented this and it feels nice. I just wish I had come across that filtered list insight on my own. I didn't know that the query with no input being fed is still '' and that filtered would work on that as every genre contains the empty string. I think in the future I will talk to myself more than talking to a chatbot - but it was genuinely useful in some cases which I will not go into right now. Anyways, I will commit all of this now and implement the bind pattern next.
